  "result": "Alright \u2014 free rein. Here's what I want to write about:\n\n**The Keeper of Holm**\n\nThe light on Holm has not been needed for nineteen years.\n\nThe Admiralty took the big lamp away in '06 and left a small solar beacon on a pole by the cottage that blinks an obedient green, every four seconds. It does the job. No ship has needed a human up there since.\n\nCalum MacIver, 74, still goes up every evening at half-past six.\n\nHe puts on his oilskin even in July \u2014 habit, and the wind up there doesn't care what month it is. He fills his thermos, picks up the big brass key that hasn't opened anything in years, and climbs the 112 steps. They're worn concave in the middle, polished by boots. His knees talk to him all the way up. He talks back.\n\nThe lantern room is empty now. The glass is cracked in one pane where a storm threw a fishing float at it in 2019 and no one ever came back to fix it. Swallows nest in the corner in summer. In winter, the wind sings through the cracks in a way that makes the whole room hum.\n\nCalum wired it himself, after Moira died. A car battery, an old inverter, a string of warm white bulbs he bought in Portree. At seven he clicks them on, and the room glows amber. It's not a signal. It doesn't reach far. It just makes a warm spot on the water where there shouldn't be one.\n\nHe sits on the upturned bucket he keeps by the window and watches the sea go black. Sometimes he writes in the logbook. Not weather reports \u2014 the coastguard stopped asking for those in 2006 \u2014 just notes for himself.\n\n*Nov 4. 6:30. Mackerel sky. Two seals by the rocks. Gulls arguing. Tea gone cold.*\n*Nov 5. 7:00. Fog. Couldn't see my boots. Tanker far out, didn't need me.*\n*Nov 6. 8:45. Clearing. Moira would have said the bulbs were wasteful. She was usually right about bulbs.*\n\nHis daughter in Aberdeen says, \"Dad, you're going to fall on those stairs one day,\" and he says, \"Stairs are stairs, love. You go up, you come down.\"\n\nHe says he'll stop when it stops feeling right. It hasn't stopped feeling right.\n\nSome nights, no one sees it. The fishing boats have GPS now. The ferries have radar. The little green beacon does all the official work. The seals don't care about amber light. The sea is just the sea, indifferent and enormous, and Holm is just a lump of rock with a cottage and a cold lantern room.\n\nHe lights it anyway.\n\nAt ten he clicks the bulbs off, closes the logbook, locks the door nobody needs locking, and climbs back down in the dark with his torch. The cottage window is still lit. The kettle's still warm. The whole island goes back to being dark.\n\nBut for three hours, the dark wasn't empty.\n\nHe does it because no one told him to.",
